{"title":"Visit Al Noor","sub":"How can a Gulf state double its international tourists?","type":"Public sector","industry":"Tourism / government","geo":"Gulf region (fictional state)","diff":2,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"35 min","skills":"Growth maths · capacity constraints · stakeholders","prompt":"The tourism authority of Al Noor, a (fictional) Gulf state, receives 5 million international visitors a year. As part of its plan to diversify the economy away from oil, the government wants 10 million visitors a year by 2033 (7 years from now). The minister has asked us what it would take.","clarify":[{"q":"Why tourism?","a":"Jobs for nationals, foreign currency, less dependence on oil revenue."},{"q":"Where do visitors come from?","a":"GCC neighbours 40%, Europe 25%, South Asia 20%, others 15%."},{"q":"Hotels?","a":"35,000 hotel rooms, ~70% occupancy. 80% of visitors stay in hotels for 4 nights on average, 1.8 guests per room."},{"q":"Pipeline?","a":"12,000 rooms under construction or approved."}],"structure":{"root":"What does it take to reach 10M visitors?","branches":[{"head":"Demand","items":["Source markets & growth","Reasons to visit: attractions, events","Marketing, visas, price"]},{"head":"Access","items":["Flights & seat capacity","Airport capacity","Visa process"]},{"head":"Capacity","items":["Hotel rooms","Holiday homes","Workforce"]},{"head":"Delivery","items":["Public vs private roles","Funding & incentives","Sustainability"]}]},"hypothesis":"Doubling in 7 years needs ~10% growth a year. That's hard but doable. I suspect hotel capacity and flights, not demand, will be the bottleneck.","questions":[{"q":"What annual growth rate is needed?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>(10 ÷ 5)^(1/7) − 1 ≈ <b>10.4% a year</b>. Rule of 72 check: 72 ÷ 10.4 ≈ 7 years to double. ✓</p>"},{"q":"How many hotel rooms will Al Noor need for 10M visitors? Assume a healthy target occupancy of 75%.","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Guest-nights = 10M × 80% × 4 = 32M → room-nights = 32M ÷ 1.8 ≈ <b>17.8M</b>.</p> <p>Rooms = 17.8M ÷ (365 × 75%) ≈ <b>65,000 rooms</b>. Today 35,000 + 12,000 pipeline = 47,000 → shortfall ≈ <b>18,000 rooms</b>.</p> <p>Sanity check on today: 5M × 0.8 × 4 ÷ 1.8 ≈ 8.9M room-nights ÷ (35,000 × 365) ≈ 70% occupancy. ✓ matches the data.</p>"},{"q":"Air capacity is the other constraint. What do you conclude?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"381.625pt\" height=\"207.636pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 381.625 207.636\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 207.636 L 381.625 207.636 L 381.625 0 L 0 0 L 0 207.636 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 28.465 182.528 L 374.425 182.528 L 374.425 27.296 L 28.465 27.296 L 28.465 182.528 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\"><g id=\"xtick_1\"><g id=\"line2d_1\"><defs><path id=\"mff7d576334\" d=\"M 0 0 L 0 3.5 \" style=\"stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></defs><g><use xlink:href=\"#mff7d576334\" x=\"77.296675\" y=\"182.528\" style=\"fill: #555555; stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_1\"><g style=\"fill: #555555\" transform=\"translate(67.607612 198.222) scale(0.09 -0.09)\"><defs><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-15\" d=\"M 2400 -64 Q 1786 -64 1338 227 Q 890 518 653 1059 Q 416 1600 416 2355 Q 416 3130 665 3680 Q 915 4230 1369 4521 Q 1824 4813 2426 4813 Q 2874 4813 3248 4633 Q 3622 4454 3868 4160 Q 4115 3866 4166 3533 Q 4173 3482 4163 3462 Q 4154 3443 4115 3430 L 3654 3360 Q 3622 3354 3606 3370 Q 3590 3386 3584 3424 Q 3539 3680 3379 3888 Q 3219 4096 2972 4221 Q 2726 4346 2419 4346 Q 1984 4346 1677 4125 Q 1370 3904 1213 3465 Q 1056 3027 1056 2394 Q 1056 1728 1213 1283 Q 1370 838 1680 620 Q 1990 403 2432 403 Q 2746 403 2976 508 Q 3206 614 3360 790 Q 3514 966 3597 1168 Q 3680 1370 3686 1562 L 3693 1946 Q 3706